\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{Functions that meet the positive eigenvector}
\date{2026-11-21}

\p{Assume the Rayleigh hypothesis, as \ref{fdip-012Y} does for
the rescaled matrix of \ref{fdip-012U}. Let the positive
eigenvector given by \ref{fdip-011X} have coordinates in that
rescaled basis. Suppose every pairing value and every coset
size is positive. The constant function, the
[double-coset kernel](fdip-012C) #{B}, and the indicator of
the stabilizer each have positive standard dot product with
that eigenvector. The stabilizer indicator differs from every
scalar multiple of the eigenvector. The products for the
constant function and for #{B} leave open whether those
functions are eigenvectors.}

\proof{
\p{\ref{fdip-012U} makes the rescaled indicators orthonormal
for counting, so the standard dot product of coordinates
equals the counting product. The constant function is the sum
of the double-coset indicators. Its coordinate on a coset of
size #{s_j} is the positive square root of #{s_j}. The
function #{B} equals #{\beta_j} on that coset, so its
coordinate is #{\beta_j} times that square root.
\ref{fdip-0114} identifies the identity double coset with the
stabilizer. The indicator of that coset equals the positive
square root of its size in that coordinate.}

\p{Each size is positive, and each pairing value is positive.
\ref{fdip-011X} makes every coordinate of the eigenvector
positive, so each of the products is a sum of products of
positive terms. \ref{fdip-0126} gives an element outside the
stabilizer, and \ref{fdip-0114} places that element in a
different double coset, so the stabilizer indicator has a
zero coordinate. A scalar multiple of the eigenvector is the
zero vector or is nonzero in every coordinate, so the
stabilizer indicator differs from every such multiple. The
constant function and #{B} are nonzero on every coset, and
the products above leave open whether either function equals
a scalar multiple of the eigenvector.}
}
